WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.833
MARK MIRCHANDANI: Active
Assist recommenders

00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:03.990
can help proactively uncover
a lot of opportunities

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.810
to improve things, including
the performance of your Cloud

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.010
workloads.

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:09.270
Imagine this scenario.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.820
You've got a web server
and an application server,

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:14.460
each hosted on Compute
Engine virtual machines.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.260
An update to the
application server

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.000
was rolled out two
weeks ago that added

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.530
a bunch of new features.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:21.118
Since then, there's
been an uptick

00:00:21.118 --> 00:00:23.160
in the number of timeout
errors on the web server

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.280
when it attempts to communicate
with the application server.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.290
It seems that performance
has been compromised,

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:30.483
and it's starting to
affect the business.

00:00:30.483 --> 00:00:32.400
How can you avoid this
scenario from happening

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:33.360
in the first place?

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.820
One way is by keeping an
eye out for opportunities

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.210
to improve performance.

00:00:37.210 --> 00:00:40.373
Let's see how Active Assist
can assist you with that.

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:41.790
It's not always
easy to figure out

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:43.457
what could be causing
performance issues

00:00:43.457 --> 00:00:45.450
and how you can
improve the situation.

00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.280
When it comes to Compute
Engine instances,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.128
an ideal place to
start is making sure

00:00:49.128 --> 00:00:50.670
that you're using
the correct machine

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.140
types for your workloads.

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.020
In the previous video, we talked
about how the VM machine type

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:57.960
recommender can help you reduce
costs by offering rightsizing

00:00:57.960 --> 00:00:59.220
recommendations.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.440
Remember, applying
rightsizing recommendations

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.140
that resize your VM to
a smaller machine type

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.210
will let you avoid
wasted resources

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.070
and optimize for costs.

00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.860
However, when resource
constraints are detected inside

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.750
of your VM, rightsizing
recommendations

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:14.790
to resize to a
larger machine type

00:01:14.790 --> 00:01:17.040
will optimize for performance.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.800
The VM machine type recommender
will recommend a larger machine

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.590
type when it identifies
a VM that is persistently

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.480
throttled by
insufficient resources,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.340
such as CPU or memory.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.270
Here, an n2-highcpu-32
offers a better fit

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:32.130
for the memory used,
as well as offering

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.100
the CPUs that are actually
needed to run the workload.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.983
In our scenario from
the start of this video,

00:01:36.983 --> 00:01:39.150
this very well might have
resolved those performance

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.350
issues.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.760
These recommendations
are created

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.490
by analyzing data from Cloud
Monitoring over the past eight

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:45.360
days.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.610
Compute Engine offers a
number of standard machine

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.370
types with fixed CPU
and memory amounts.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.530
If none of those fit the
requirements identified

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.870
by a recommender, a
custom machine type

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:56.580
might be offered with
appropriate amounts

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.320
of CPU and memory.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.910
Rightsizing
recommendations aren't

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.770
just limited to single
instances, either.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.560
You can also receive rightsizing
recommendations for VMs

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.540
in a managed instance group.

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:09.090
These recommendations will come
from the appropriately named

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:11.580
Managed Instance Group
machine type recommender,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:13.800
but they work very much
like the single instance

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:14.910
recommendations.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:17.387
This is especially useful
for cases where you may not

00:02:17.387 --> 00:02:19.470
be able to increase the
size of the instance group

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:23.100
or for workloads that just don't
work well with auto-scaling.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.810
For Managed Instance
Groups, metrics

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.340
are collected and
analyzed from all

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.780
of the instances in the group.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:29.280
That way, the
recommendation won't

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.890
undersize for any of the
individual instances.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.260
As always, you know
your workloads best.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.060
When you receive
those recommendations,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.530
you can choose to
review and apply

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.600
them or just dismiss
them and hide them

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:40.860
from the Cloud Console.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:42.510
You can always view
them in the History

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.120
page of the Recommendation Hub.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.770
Beyond just virtual
machines, there's

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:48.270
a few recommenders
that can help you

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:51.490
maintain or improve performance
of other Cloud resources.

00:02:51.490 --> 00:02:53.640
For example, if you're
using Cloud SQL,

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.890
you can receive recommendations
from the Cloud SQL

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:57.625
out-of-disk recommender.

00:02:57.625 --> 00:03:00.000
These recommendations come
from looking at the disk space

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.040
usage of a Cloud SQL instance.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.290
By analyzing the storage
utilization trends

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.480
over the last 30
days, recommendations

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.775
can be generated if an instance
is likely to run out of space.

00:03:09.775 --> 00:03:11.400
The recommendations
will suggest either

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.380
manually increasing
storage capacity,

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:16.290
enabling the ability to
automatically increase storage,

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:19.200
or deleting data to
free up disk space.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:21.390
Active Assist can help
you be proactive and avoid

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:22.947
downtime or
performance issues that

00:03:22.947 --> 00:03:25.530
could be caused by running out
of disk space on your Cloud SQL

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:27.120
instances.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.190
Performance issues can
be tricky to deal with.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:30.783
The recommenders
we've looked at today

00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:32.700
can help you stay on top
of performance issues

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.370
by analyzing usage data
and suggesting ways

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.810
to fix resource constraints.

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:37.980
You can check out
the documentation

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:39.390
to try these out for
yourself, and don't

00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:40.860
forget about the
Recommendation Hub

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.530
where you can see all sorts
of helpful recommendations.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.030
Stay tuned for the
next video, where

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.410
we'll look at some more
recommendations that

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.780
will focus around strengthening
security in your Cloud

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.560
environment.

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:51.470
See you there.

